  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>A layered process audit (LPA) is a short, repeated check of the same critical process points, run by multiple layers of the organisation at different frequencies</li><li>An LPA verifies adherence to standardised work, not the quality of the finished part; final inspection is a separate thing</li><li>LPAs originated in automotive quality and are common across IATF 16949 supply chains, though any manufacturer benefits</li><li>The value is frequency and follow-through; a back-dated audit and a finding with no corrective action are both worthless</li><li>Per-report pricing beats per-seat for a plant where dozens of auditors run checks, because adding auditors is free</li></ul></div><h2>What a layered process audit actually checks</h2><p>A layered process audit is a short verification that a defined process is being carried out exactly as the standardised work document says. Typical items are concrete and binary: is the correct fixture in use, is the torque wrench in calibration, is the operator following the sequence, is the gauge in date, is PPE worn, is the first-off signed. Each takes seconds at the station.</p><p>The word layered matters. The same critical points are audited by several layers of the organisation: team leaders often daily, supervisors and quality staff weekly, senior managers monthly. Layering catches drift a single auditor would normalise, and it puts leadership on the floor. Software does not improve the process; the audit, done honestly and acted upon, does.</p><h2>An LPA is not a final inspection</h2><p>A final inspection or quality gate judges the part: is this component within tolerance. A layered process audit judges the process: is the operator doing the job the way the standard says, so that the parts come out right in the first place. An LPA is a leading indicator. A drifting process shows up in the audit before it shows up as scrap or a customer complaint. That is why the questions are about method, setup and controls rather than measurements of the product.</p><h2>Automotive roots and IATF 16949</h2><p>Layered process audits grew out of automotive manufacturing, where OEMs pushed the practice down their supply chains to hold standardised work in place. Suppliers operating to IATF 16949, the automotive quality management standard, commonly run LPA programmes, and customer-specific requirements often mandate them. The practice is not limited to automotive; any manufacturer running standardised work uses the same shape. Confirm the current version of IATF 16949 and your customer's own audit requirements directly rather than relying on a template.</p><h2>The corrective action loop</h2><p>An LPA finding that never becomes a corrective action is worse than no audit, because it records a problem nobody addressed. The value is in the loop: the deviation is found, an action is raised and owned, and a later audit confirms it stuck. Repeated fails on the same point signal that the standardised work itself, or the training behind it, needs changing. Quickler opens a corrective action the moment an auditor logs a fail and keeps the full trail.</p><h2>What to look for in an LPA tool</h2><ul><li><strong>On-schedule prompting:</strong> the audit should arrive when it is due, so frequency does not slip when the floor is busy</li><li><strong>No back-dating:</strong> a timestamped record makes pencil-whipping a week of checks impossible</li><li><strong>Corrective action loop:</strong> a fail should open an owned action, not just a red tick</li><li><strong>No per-seat penalty:</strong> per-seat pricing taxes you for running the programme at every layer. Quickler charges per report, unlimited users on every bundle</li><li><strong>Evidence trail:</strong> auditor, layer, station, deviation, photo and outcome, for the customer auditor who asks later</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> Quickler runs the audit and manages the corrective-action trail. It does not design your standardised work, set your audit schedule or replace the judgement of your quality team. This page is general information, not compliance advice; confirm the current IATF 16949 requirements and your customer's own rules. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if that distinction matters for your firm.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is a layered process audit?</h3><p>A layered process audit (LPA) is a short, frequent check that a defined process is being carried out exactly as its standardised work document specifies. The same critical points are audited by several layers of the organisation, from team leaders daily to senior managers monthly. It verifies adherence to the process rather than inspecting the finished part.</p><h3>How is an LPA different from a final inspection?</h3><p>A final inspection judges the product: is the part within tolerance. A layered process audit judges the process: is the operator following the standard so that parts come out right. An LPA is a leading indicator; a drifting process shows up in the audit before it shows up as scrap or a customer return.</p><h3>Are layered process audits required by IATF 16949?</h3><p>LPAs are strongly associated with automotive quality and IATF 16949 supply chains, and specific customers often mandate them contractually. Whether they are required in your case depends on your customers and the standards you work to. Confirm the current version of IATF 16949 and your customer's own audit requirements directly.</p><h3>Can I run layered process audits over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's LPA workflow runs over the WhatsApp Business API. The audit arrives as a message when it is due, the auditor answers at the line with text, a voice note or a photo, and a fail opens a corrective action the office sees immediately. No separate app or login is required, and Quickler manages the WhatsApp Business API account on the firm's behalf.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/forklift-inspection-checklist-uk.html\">Forklift inspection checklist</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/factory-audit-software-uk.html\">Factory audit software</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/5s-workplace-audit-app-uk.html\">5S workplace audit app</a></li></ul>",
